1
00:03:44,117 --> 00:03:50,117
An adventure 65 million years in the making.

2
00:07:28,235 --> 00:07:34,235
Jurassic Park is a adventure, science fiction film directed by Steven Spielberg.

3
00:11:12,352 --> 00:11:18,352
A wealthy entrepreneur secretly creates a theme park featuring
living dinosaurs drawn from prehistoric DNA.

4
00:14:56,470 --> 00:15:02,470
Before opening day, he invites a team of experts and his two eager
grandchildren to experience the park and help calm anxious investors.

5
00:18:40,588 --> 00:18:46,588
However, the park is anything but amusing as the security
systems go off-line and the dinosaurs escape.

6
00:22:24,705 --> 00:22:30,705
Grant, played by Sam Neill.

7
00:26:08,823 --> 00:26:14,823
Ellie, played by Laura Dern.

8
00:29:52,941 --> 00:29:58,941
Malcolm, played by Jeff Goldblum.

9
00:33:37,058 --> 00:33:43,058
Hammond, played by Richard Attenborough.

10
00:37:21,176 --> 00:37:27,176
Muldoon, played by Bob Peck.

11
00:41:05,294 --> 00:41:11,294
Gennaro, played by Martin Ferrero.

12
00:44:49,411 --> 00:44:55,411
Jurassic Park is a 1993 American science fiction adventure film directed by Steven Spielberg
and written by Michael Crichton and David Koepp, based on Crichton's 1990 novel.

13
00:48:33,529 --> 00:48:39,529
Starring Sam Neill, Laura Dern, Jeff Goldblum, and Richard Attenborough, the film is set on the fictional island of Isla Nublar near Costa Rica, where
wealthy businessman John Hammond (Attenborough) and a team of genetic scientists have created a wildlife park of de-extinct dinosaurs.

14
00:52:17,647 --> 00:52:23,647
When industrial sabotage leads to a catastrophic shutdown of the park's power facilities and security
precautions, a small group of visitors struggle to survive and escape the now perilous island.

15
00:56:01,764 --> 00:56:07,764
Before Crichton's novel was published, four studios
put in bids for its film rights.

16
00:59:45,882 --> 00:59:51,882
With the backing of Universal Pictures, Spielberg
acquired the rights for $1.5 million.

17
01:03:30,000 --> 01:03:36,000
Crichton was hired for an additional $500,000 to adapt the novel for the screen.

18
01:07:14,117 --> 01:07:20,117
Koepp wrote the final draft, which left out much of the novel's exposition
and violence, while making numerous changes to the characters.

19
01:10:58,235 --> 01:11:04,235
Filming took place in California and Hawaii from August to November 1992, and post-production
lasted until May 1993, supervised by Spielberg in Poland as he filmed Schindler's List.

20
01:14:42,352 --> 01:14:48,352
The dinosaurs were created with groundbreaking computer-generated imagery by Industrial Light &
Magic, and with life-sized animatronic dinosaurs built by Stan Winston's team.

21
01:18:26,470 --> 01:18:32,470
To showcase the film's sound design, which included a mixture of various animal noises for the dinosaur sounds,
Spielberg invested in the creation of DTS, a company specializing in digital surround sound formats.

22
01:22:10,588 --> 01:22:16,588
The film was backed by an extensive $65 million marketing campaign,
which included licensing deals with over 100 companies.

23
01:25:54,705 --> 01:26:00,705
Jurassic Park premiered on June 9, 1993, at the Uptown Theater in Washington,
D.C., and was released two days later throughout the United States.

24
01:29:38,823 --> 01:29:44,823
It was a blockbuster hit and went on to gross over $914 million worldwide in its original
theatrical run, becoming the highest-grossing film of 1993, surpassing Spielberg's own E.T.

25
01:33:22,941 --> 01:33:28,941
the Extra-Terrestrial to become the highest-grossing film of all time until
the release of Titanic (1997), surpassing it in early 1998.

26
01:37:07,058 --> 01:37:13,058
The film was praised for its special effects, sound design, action
sequences, John Williams's score, and Spielberg's direction.

27
01:40:51,176 --> 01:40:57,176
It won 20 awards, including three Academy Awards for technical
achievements in visual effects and sound design.

28
01:44:35,294 --> 01:44:41,294
Following its 20th anniversary re-release in 2013, Jurassic Park became the oldest film
in history to surpass $1 billion in ticket sales and the 17th overall.

29
01:48:19,411 --> 01:48:25,411
Jurassic Park's pioneering use of computer-generated imagery is considered to have
paved the way for the special effects practices of modern cinema.

30
01:52:03,529 --> 01:52:09,529
In 2018, it was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

31
01:55:47,647 --> 01:55:53,647
The film spawned a multimedia franchise that includes six sequels, video games,
theme park attractions, comic books, and various merchandise.

32
01:59:31,764 --> 01:59:37,764
The film explores themes of exotic island, island,
triceratops, brontosaurus, electric fence.

33
02:03:15,882 --> 02:03:21,882
The tone is lighthearted and thought_provoking.
